=document :!auto-toc
=begin rakudoc
=TITLE Tutorial #2 format codes

In this article I will be discussing some of the markup codes which can be used in a RakuDoc source. 

=head Comparison of styling using other systems

It is very useful for a reader when the author lays out text in a pleasant manner, including spaces
between paragraphs, and styling words within paragraphs to draw attention to them. 

=nested
Practices that at first are considered considerate, become mandatory over time. For example, the very
first styling of text came in Roman times, when good practice was to separate the letters making up words
with spaces, and then to use punctuation marks. Now it is impossible to eliminate spaces and poor
punctuation is a sign of a poor education. L<Here is an interesting article on punctuation| https://www.bbc.co.uk/culture/article/20150902-the-mysterious-origins-of-punctuation>.

Let's consider the ways in which we can highlight a word by making it B<bolder>.

In an MS Word document, an author will highlight the words to be emboldened and then use C<Ctrl-B> or the
equivalent on a Mac. This is a quick and easy way of styling, unless the author decides too many bold
words exist and wants to restyle to I<Italic>. Then it becomes a hassle to change.

Using MarkDown, it is much easier in that the words to be styled are 'bracketed' using special
characters, so C<** ... **> are used to embolden, while C<* ... *> are used for italicising. There are a
few other characters such as `` for code. But as soon as you need more sophisticated styling, such as
pushing the characters up as in exponentiation, or down, it's necessary to use HTML tags. However, the
upside is that unlike an WYSIWYG editor, like Word, changing the styling is easy: change the styling
character. And yet ... each different type of style, or rather its style character, has to be learnt.

=head A declarative approach

RakuDoc approaches styling in a different way: a number of markup codes are defined in a declarative
manner, and all use the same syntax, C<Upper case letter> C<opener>contentC<closer> e.g., 
C<B<a basic term>>. 

The simplest RakuDoc markup codes enclose text and provide a rendering, and they are:
=begin section
=config C :allow<R V>
=begin table :!toc :caption('')
    =row :header
        =cell Inline markup
        =cell Instruction
        =cell Mnemonic
        =cell HTML equivalent
        =cell Markdown equivalent
    =row
        =cell Basis
        =cell C«V<B><R<text>>»
        =cell I<“B for Basis”>
        =cell C«<strong>R<text></strong>»
        =cell C«**R<text>**»
    =row
        =cell Important
        =cell C«V<I><R<text>>»
        =cell I<“I for Important”>
        =cell C«<em>R<text></em>»
        =cell C«*R<text>*»
    =row
        =cell Unusual
        =cell C«V<U><R<text>>»
        =cell I<“U for Unusual”>
        =cell C«<ins>R<text></ins>»
        =cell C«<ins>R<text></ins>»
    =row
        =cell Weighty
        =cell C«V<W><R<text>>»
        =cell I<“W for Weighty”>
        =cell C«V«<»span style="font-variant: small-caps;">R<text>V«<»/span>»
        =cell C«V«<»span style="font-variant: small-caps;">R<text>V«<»/span>»
    =row
        =cell Struck out
        =cell C«V<O><R<text>>»
        =cell I<“O for Out”>
        =cell C«<del>R<text></del>»
        =cell C«~~R<text>~~»
    =row
        =cell Superscript
        =cell C«V<H><R<text>>»
        =cell I<“H for High text”>
        =cell C«<sup>R<text></sup>»
        =cell C«<sup>R<text></sup>»
    =row
        =cell Subscript
        =cell C«V<J><R<text>>»
        =cell I<“J for Junior text”>
        =cell C«<sub>R<text></sub>»
        =cell C«<sub>R<text></sub>»
    =row
        =cell Code
        =cell C«V<C><R<text>>»
        =cell I<“C for Code”>
        =cell C«<code>R<text></code>»
        =cell C«`R<text>`»

=end table
=end section

The aim of the naming system is to provide a hint about the rendering, while leaving it to the renderer
to implement the final output format.

The same syntax is used for other types of inline content. Almost all the Latin uppercase letters, and a
Greek uppercase letter have been defined for standard RakuDoc. Rather than describing each of the codes,
it is probably best just to look at the specification document included in the samples.

However, the formatting markup syntax is not limited to the specified letters, and any Unicode character with the
Uppercase property can be used for customised content. For example, the C<Rakuast::RakuDoc::Renderer>
comes with a custom markup for inserting Font Awesome icons into the text. L<Some examples are given in
the documentation for the module|https://finanalyst.github.io/plugins/FontAwesome>.

The aim of these initial articles is to provide an introduction, rather than give an extensive tutorial.
The best way to see how the markup codes work is to try them in the browser.

Try copying the following RakuDoc source into this document but without the C<=begin code>/C<=end code> lines.
=begin code
This is some B<basic> highlighting showing C<my $new code> with 
SomeJ<B<junior>>andH<I<higher>> text.

Here are some FontAwesome icons (ℱ = U+2131):

Train normal size ℱ<fas fa-train> Triple size train ℱ<fas fa-train fa-3x>

An animated spinner ℱ<fas fa-sync fa-spin fa-3x fa-fw>

Here is an inline image P<https://cdn.esahubble.org/archives/images/screen/potw2552a.jpg>
=end code

{ copy the code here }

Click on the framework options in this editor to see how the online version is better than the minimal version.

In the minimal framework, you will find some warnings at the end of the rendered version about errors found
during the rendering. The simple HTML renderer is designed to be used offline and all the CSS and
Javascript are contained inside the file.

However, we now expect online connections with CSS, images and JS libraries delivered using CDNs. So the
HTML-Extra renderer uses the Bulma CSS framework and has plugins to pull in FontAwesome and the
LeafletMaps library (we will cover this in a future article).

Now click on the C<Go on line> button. It may take some moments to render and pull the image in from the
Hubble telescope site.
=end rakudoc